  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased speed and volume of reporting and warnings provides earlier detection cues and actionable intelligence to air/missile-defense operators, improving interception timing and asset allocation.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_regional_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_energy_chokepoint</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_regional_war::information_flow" target="russia_ukraine_war::cyber_intrusion_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An increased volume and velocity of public reporting and operational chatter about the US–Iran war creates more exploitable situational noise and phishing/cover narratives that hostile actors use to raise the frequency and success of cyber intrusions against networks linked to the Russia–Ukraine conflict.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_regional_war</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_iran_regional_war::information_flow" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::media_attention" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A faster, higher-volume stream of reports, leaks, and commentary about the US–Iran confrontation draws newsroom resources and social amplification, increasing coverage that explicitly links the conflict to great‑power tech competition and boosting media attention on the US–China tech rivalry.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-3 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_iran_regional_war</data>
